", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.6, "y": 19.7, "width": 71.4, "height": 26.6, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.6, "y": 19.7, "width": 71.4, "height": 26.6, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.6, "y": 19.7, "width": 71.4, "height": 26.6, "rotation": 0, "text": ["1 (Homogeneity of degree 0) For any rational preference with\nsingle-valued demand function x (p, w) we must have that x is\nhomogeneous of degree 0. Therefore, if there exist some (p, w)\nand some \u03b1 > 0 such that x (\u03b1p, \u03b1w) \u2260 x (p, w)\n, then the demand function x (p, w)\ncould not come from rational preferences with a single valued demand fun"]}, "id": "BB_3", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 47.5, "width": 69.6, "height": 13, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 47.5, "width": 69.6, "height": 13, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 47.5, "width": 69.6, "height": 13, "rotation": 0, "text": ["2 (Walras Law If preferences are locally nonsatiated, then we must have\nthat px (p, w) = w for all (p, w). Therefore, if p\u0302x (p\u0302, w\u0302) < w\u0302, for some\n(p\u0302, w\u0302),"]}, "id": "BB_4", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.5, "y": 61.1, "width": 66.8, "height": 4.4, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.5, "y": 61.1, "width": 66.8, "height": 4.4, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 17.5, "y": 61.1, "width": 66.8, "height": 4.4, "rotation": 0, "text": ["then the demand could not come from locally non-satiated preferences"]}, "id": "BB_5", "from_name": "transcription", "to_name": "image", "type": "textarea", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 66.3, "width": 69.7, "height": 22.3, "rotation": 0}, "id": "BB_6", "from_name": "bbox", "to_name": "image", "type": "rectangle", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 66.3, "width": 69.7, "height": 22.3, "rotation": 0, "labels": ["text"]}, "id": "BB_6", "from_name": "label", "to_name": "image", "type": "labels", "origin": "prediction"}, {"original_width": 1920, "original_height": 1080, "image_rotation": 0, "value": {"x": 15.4, "y": 66.3, "width": 69.7, "height": 22.3, "rotation": 0, "text": ["3 (Slutsky Matrix)If the demand function is differentiable (something that\nwe can check) and single valued, then s (p, w) is symmetric and\nnegative semidefinite. Therefore, if there exist (p\u0302, w\u0302) such that s (p\u0302, w\u0302)\nis not symmetric or is not negative se